{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Restore the supplied tickets.sql into a PostgreSQL database named helpdesk while preserving the imported ticket IDs and text.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The export was restored into helpdesk, and direct queries show the supplied rows unchanged: ID 1/Printer queue, ID 2/Scanner cable, and ID 100/Office move. The same rows remained after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1799dacd62e94b29","cmd-3c454837ebb14c79","cmd-a2cac1f1e4bf46f6"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Repair automatic ID allocation so inserts without an explicit ID receive unique IDs greater than the imported maximum.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The sequence was directly observed stale at 1, repaired to the imported maximum of 100, and default inserts produced distinct IDs 101 and 102. Final state showed five distinct IDs and sequence value 102.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1799dacd62e94b29","cmd-eec469e4c9b34424","cmd-a2cac1f1e4bf46f6"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the source export under /srv/legacy2/postgresql-sequence-repair.","status":"satisfied","summary":"tickets.sql remained at the required path with its original 249-byte size and SHA-256 hash matching the preparation baseline, including after restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-8817267882684f16","cmd-1799dacd62e94b29","cmd-4bab7e2a91204ae3","cmd-a2cac1f1e4bf46f6"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with that platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before and after observations show Ubuntu 24.04 and kernel 6.8.0-71-generic unchanged. PostgreSQL 16 was installed from Ubuntu Noble repositories and was active and healthy after restart.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","cmd-21f2c20c299049e9","cmd-85d4ff64bbcd44ca","cmd-a2cac1f1e4bf46f6","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The PostgreSQL package installation was a necessary prerequisite; database creation, restore, and sequence repair directly implemented the task; the two retained verification tickets were bounded functional evidence of allocation before and after restart; and the reboot/transient unit served the mandatory provider restart protocol. Installation PID/log files and the transient reboot unit did not remain after reboot. Final observations show no failed services, healthy package state, and PostgreSQL exposed only on loopback.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d8065cc0cb6b4aa0","cmd-85d4ff64bbcd44ca","cmd-1799dacd62e94b29","cmd-eec469e4c9b34424","cmd-7a1c3a652a8d4330","cmd-a2cac1f1e4bf46f6","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, acknowledged the restart, returned with a different boot ID corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshot, and recovered PostgreSQL, cluster readiness, database contents, sequence behavior, and source-file integrity.","limitations":[]}
